= a
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

It is essential to distinguish a finite-dimensional spinor matrix from its action on a family of operator components. Take the <Pauli matrices> and $\bar\sigma^{\mu\nu}$ in the supplied convention, with $\eta=\operatorname{diag}(1,-1,-1,-1)$. They give
$$
\bar\sigma^{ij}=\frac12\epsilon_{ijk}\sigma_k,
\qquad\bar\sigma^{0i}=\frac i2\sigma_i.
$$
If both displayed transformations are read literally as left multiplication on the column $\bar Q^{\dot\alpha}$, expanding the operator conjugation and comparing its linear term gives the formal answer $[M^{\mu\nu},\bar Q]=+\bar\sigma^{\mu\nu}\bar Q$.

There is a sign inconsistency in that literal reading. With the usual <angular momentum commutation relations>, coefficient matrices $R_i$ in $[J_i,q_a]=(R_i)_{ab}q_b$ must satisfy
$$
[R_i,R_j]=-i\epsilon_{ijk}R_k.
$$
Indeed, the <Jacobi identity> gives $[J_i,[J_j,q]]-[J_j,[J_i,q]]=(R_jR_i-R_iR_j)q$, whereas $[[J_i,J_j],q]=i\epsilon_{ijk}R_kq$. The formal $R_i=+\sigma_i/2$ violates this identity. For example it would give $[J_3,q_1]=q_1/2$, $[J_+,q_1]=q_2$ and $[J_3,q_2]=-q_2/2$; the two sides of the <Jacobi identity> for $J_3,J_+,q_1$ are then $-q_2/2$ and $3q_2/2$.

A consistent canonical column-operator convention uses the inverse adjoint action for the displayed spinor transformation, equivalently the contragredient operator-component prescription. It gives
$$
\boxed{[M^{\mu\nu},\bar Q^{\dot\alpha}]
=-(\bar\sigma^{\mu\nu})^{\dot\alpha}{}_{\dot\beta}\bar Q^{\dot\beta}.}
$$
This is the convention used for the physical states below. Reversing the adjoint action is the minimum needed sign correction in the literal column reading; a dual row notation can encode the same correction by its index contractions. The formal printed-sign coefficients are also recorded below, so the distinction is explicit rather than silently changing a convention. The representation matrices themselves remain $J_i=\sigma_i/2$ and $K_i=i\sigma_i/2$ in Question 3: the minus sign here concerns <commutators> of operator components.

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

A <supercharge> is an odd <Weyl spinor> generator. The mixed <anticommutator> has one undotted and one dotted index, hence transforms as a <Lorentz four-vector>. In the minimal <Super-Poincaré algebra>, its only spacetime generator is the <four-momentum>, so covariance fixes it to $c\sigma^\mu_{\alpha\dot\beta}P_\mu$. Normalizing the charges canonically fixes $c=2$:
$$
\boxed{\{Q_\alpha,\bar Q_{\dot\beta}\}
=2\sigma^\mu_{\alpha\dot\beta}P_\mu.}
$$
The number two is a normalization, not something Lorentz covariance alone could fix under $Q\mapsto aQ$.

For an explicit closure calculation, use left <Grassmann derivatives> in <superspace> and the differential translation convention $P_\mu=i\partial_\mu$:
$$
Q_\alpha=\frac\partial{\partial\theta^\alpha}
-i\sigma^\mu_{\alpha\dot\beta}\bar\theta^{\dot\beta}\partial_\mu,
\qquad
\bar Q_{\dot\beta}=-\frac\partial{\partial\bar\theta^{\dot\beta}}
+i\theta^\alpha\sigma^\mu_{\alpha\dot\beta}\partial_\mu.
$$
Each of the two derivative-coordinate cross terms contributes $i\sigma^\mu\partial_\mu$; the other terms cancel by anticommutativity. This proves the displayed coefficient. The same calculation gives $\{Q,Q\}=\{\bar Q,\bar Q\}=0$ and $[P_\mu,Q]=[P_\mu,\bar Q]=0$. There is no scalar central charge for a single <supersymmetry> label: symmetry of the <anticommutator> is incompatible with a term proportional only to the antisymmetric $\epsilon_{\alpha\beta}$.

In the massive rest frame $P_\mu=(m,0,0,0)$, the mixed relation is $\{Q_\alpha,Q_\beta^\dagger\}=2m\delta_{\alpha\beta}$. These positive <canonical anticommutation relations> supply the input for the normalization and finite <fermionic Fock space> in the question-level continuation.

= Solution
{parent=1}

Write $q_1=\bar Q^{\dot1}$ and $q_2=\bar Q^{\dot2}$. In the Jacobi-consistent convention just fixed,
$$
[J_i,q_a]=-\frac12(\sigma_i)_{ab}q_b.
$$
Applying the <commutator> product rule twice gives
$$
[J^2,q_a]=\sum_i\bigl(J_i[J_i,q_a]+[J_i,q_a]J_i\bigr)
=\frac34q_a-\sum_i(\sigma_i)_{ab}q_bJ_i.
$$
Here $\sum_i\sigma_i^2=3I$ produces the constant term; moving the $J_i$ to the right accounts for it. Consequently
$$
\begin{aligned}
[J^2,q_1]&=\tfrac34q_1-q_2(J_1-iJ_2)-q_1J_3,\\
[J^2,q_2]&=\tfrac34q_2-q_1(J_1+iJ_2)+q_2J_3.
\end{aligned}
$$
Thus the physically consistent numerical coefficients are
$$
\boxed{(a_1,\ldots,a_9)=
(-\tfrac12,\tfrac34,-1,i,-1,\tfrac34,-1,-i,1).}
$$
If one mechanically retains the literal positive-sign column transformation instead, the coefficient tuple would be $(\tfrac12,\tfrac34,1,-i,1,\tfrac34,1,i,-1)$. It cannot describe a unitary multiplet with the supplied rotation algebra, by the explicit Jacobi counterexample in part (a).

Take the spin-zero <Clifford vacuum> $|\Omega\rangle$, normalized to one and annihilated by the two annihilation charges. This is a lowest Fock state of a positive-mass representation, not a zero-energy vacuum annihilated by every charge. For the first one-charge state,
$$
J^2q_1|\Omega\rangle=\tfrac34q_1|\Omega\rangle,
\qquad J_3q_1|\Omega\rangle=-\tfrac12q_1|\Omega\rangle.
$$
The ladder <commutators> are $[J_+,q_1]=-q_2$, $[J_-,q_1]=0$, $[J_+,q_2]=0$, $[J_-,q_2]=-q_1$. Therefore these two states are the $j=1/2$ pair. A phase choice $|\tfrac12,+\tfrac12\rangle=-Nq_2|\Omega\rangle$ makes the usual positive ladder coefficient hold.

Raising the dotted index only changes the oscillator basis by the unitary antisymmetric epsilon matrix, so $\{q_a,q_b^\dagger\}=2m\delta_{ab}$. Since $q_a^\dagger|\Omega\rangle=0$,
$$
\|q_a|\Omega\rangle\|^2=2m,
\qquad\boxed{N=\frac1{\sqrt{2m}},\quad Nq_1|\Omega\rangle:
(j,j_3)=(\tfrac12,-\tfrac12).}
$$
The positive convention would formally label this state with $j_3=+1/2$ while $J_+$ acts nontrivially on it, contradicting the highest-weight ladder rule. This supplies an independent state-level check of the needed sign correction.

The two-charge state is a <rotation singlet>. Indeed, using $q_1^2=q_2^2=0$ and $q_2q_1=-q_1q_2$, the product rule gives $[J_i,q_1q_2]=0$ for every $i$: only the sum of the two diagonal spinor coefficients survives, and their trace is zero. Hence the requested <commutators> and quantum numbers are
$$
\boxed{[J^2,q_1q_2]=[J_3,q_1q_2]=0,
\qquad N^2q_1q_2|\Omega\rangle:(j,j_3)=(0,0).}
$$
Its squared norm is $(2m)^2$ before multiplication by $N^4$, by the two oscillator <anticommutators>.

The <spin-zero massive N=1 supermultiplet> therefore consists of $|\Omega\rangle$, $Nq_1|\Omega\rangle$, $Nq_2|\Omega\rangle$ and $N^2q_1q_2|\Omega\rangle$. Their <spins> are $0,1/2,1/2,0$, all with the same mass $m$. Starting with an even vacuum, the two spin-zero states are bosonic and the two one-charge states fermionic. They are the two real scalar degrees of freedom and the two on-shell <fermion> polarizations of a massive chiral multiplet. Any product of three creation charges repeats an index and vanishes; annihilation charges reduce to these four states by the <canonical anticommutation relations>. **There are exactly four independent states.**

= 2
{parent=Paper 45}
{scope}

= a
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Assume the standard canonical kinetic action $\int d^4\theta\,\Phi^\dagger\Phi$. A constant <field redefinition> $\Phi=\Psi+s$ is invertible and preserves <chirality>. Its kinetic action differs by $s\Psi^\dagger+s^*\Psi+|s|^2$, whose full <superspace> integrals vanish up to a boundary term: a chiral or antichiral field has no independent full-superspace component. Equivalently, spacetime derivatives of a constant are zero. There is no nontrivial Jacobian for this translation of the field. Thus physical predictions are unchanged when all parameters and the coordinate of the vacuum are transformed together.

The transformed <superpotential> is
$$
W(\Psi+s)=A+\left(\kappa+ms+\frac g2s^2\right)\Psi
+\frac{m+gs}{2}\Psi^2+\frac g6\Psi^3,
$$
where $A=W(s)$. Removing the linear term means choosing a critical point $W'(s)=0$, not merely choosing $s=-\kappa/m$ while ignoring the cubic term. For $g\ne0$,
$$
\boxed{s=\frac{-m\pm\sqrt\Delta}{g},\qquad
\Delta=m^2-2g\kappa,\qquad M:=m+gs=\pm\sqrt\Delta.}
$$
The resulting <superpotential> is $A+M\Psi^2/2+g\Psi^3/6$. Its constant term has no effect in global <supersymmetry>, since its chiral <superspace> integral is zero. It would matter in <supergravity>, which is not the model here.

A real shift is physically allowed, but it does not always suffice to remove the linear term. With real couplings it requires $\Delta\geq0$ for $g\ne0$. For example $m=0$, $\kappa=1$, $g=2$ gives $W'(s)=1+s^2$, which has no real root. A complex shift $s=\pm i$ is still a valid redefinition of the complex chiral scalar and removes the term. Thus the printed instruction needs either the real-root hypothesis or permission for a complex shift.

If $g=0$ and $m\ne0$, choose $s=-\kappa/m$. If $g=m=0$, no shift removes a nonzero $\kappa$; if $\kappa=0$ it is already absent. These exceptional branches will also distinguish the supersymmetry-breaking possibilities below.

= b
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Eliminating the <auxiliary field> gives $F^*=-W'(\varphi)$ and the nonnegative <F-term scalar potential>
$$
\boxed{V(\varphi)=\left|\kappa+m\varphi+\frac g2\varphi^2\right|^2.}
$$
Use $u=\varphi-s$ after the critical-point shift, so that
$$
\boxed{V(u)=\left|Mu+\frac g2u^2\right|^2.}
$$
This notation keeps the original parameters distinct from the shifted mass coefficient $M$. For real $M,g$ with $Mg\ne0$, the real slice is $V(u)=u^2(M+gu/2)^2$. It has zero-energy minima at $u=0$ and $u=-2M/g$, a maximum between them at $u=-M/g$ with height $M^4/(4g^2)$, and grows quartically toward both ends. These statements follow by differentiating: $V'(u)=2u(M+gu/2)(M+gu)$.

When $M=0$ it becomes $|g|^2u^4/4$, with one degenerate zero minimum. When $g=0$, it is $|M|^2u^2$ after a permissible shift. For the real parameters with negative <discriminant>, the original real slice can have a positive minimum even though the full complex <scalar potential> has zero-energy vacua away from that slice. The sketch distinguishes these regimes:

\Image[paper-45-scalar-potentials.png]
{height=660}
{title=Real scalar-potential slices: two supersymmetric minima, a double root, complex vacua off the slice, and flat F-term breaking}

The plots use illustrative normalized coefficients, not values fixed by the exam. **A positive minimum on a chosen real slice is not evidence for <supersymmetry breaking> in the full complex field space.**

= c
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

A <supersymmetric vacuum> satisfies $F=0$, equivalently $W'(v)=0$, and has $V(v)=0$. For $g\ne0$, the nonconstant quadratic $W'$ has at least one complex root, including a double root when $\Delta=0$. Since the scalar is complex, these roots are admissible physical vacua whether or not they lie on the real slice. Thus <supersymmetry> is unbroken at either zero of $W'$. The same is true for $g=0,m\ne0$, where $W'$ is linear and has the root $v=-\kappa/m$.

The exceptional pure-linear model $g=m=0,\kappa\ne0$ has $F^*=-\kappa$ everywhere and $V=|\kappa|^2>0$. It exhibits <flat F-term breaking with a linear superpotential>: all scalar values are flat vacua but no state annihilates every <supercharge>. If also $\kappa=0$, the potential is identically zero and <supersymmetry> is unbroken on a flat moduli space.

Consequently **the generic cubic or massive model does not spontaneously break <supersymmetry>; only the nonzero purely linear branch does in this single-field canonical model.** The answer is independent of $\alpha$.

= d
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

In the original coordinate, the possible zero-energy <vacuum expectation values> for $g\ne0$ are
$$
\boxed{v_\pm=\frac{-m\pm\sqrt{m^2-2g\kappa}}{g}.}
$$
They coincide when the <discriminant> vanishes. With real parameters and a nonnegative <discriminant> these are real; otherwise the allowed <complex scalar> values must be retained.

In the coordinate $u=\varphi-s$ used to remove the linear term, the same vacua are
$$
\boxed{\langle u\rangle=0\quad\text{or}\quad-\frac{2M}{g}.}
$$
The nonzero branch exists in this shifted coordinate when $M\ne0$. Let $w=-2M/g$ be that branch and write $u=w+\widetilde\varphi$. Equivalently, in the original coordinate write $\varphi=v+\widetilde\varphi$ with $v=s+w$. The physical fluctuation field is the same. For $g=0,m\ne0$ there is the unique vacuum $v=-\kappa/m$; for the constant and pure-linear exceptional branches the scalar is a flat direction, with the <supersymmetry> status already distinguished in part (c).

= e
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

Taylor expansion about any <supersymmetric vacuum> $v$ gives
$$
W'(v+\widetilde\varphi)
=W'(v)+W''(v)\widetilde\varphi+\frac g2\widetilde\varphi^2
=M_v\widetilde\varphi+\frac g2\widetilde\varphi^2,
\qquad M_v=m+gv.
$$
Therefore the fluctuation <scalar potential> is
$$
\boxed{V(\widetilde\varphi)
=|M_v|^2|\widetilde\varphi|^2
+\frac12\left(M_v^*g\,\widetilde\varphi^*\widetilde\varphi^2
+M_vg^*\,\widetilde\varphi(\widetilde\varphi^*)^2\right)
+\frac{|g|^2}{4}|\widetilde\varphi|^4.}
$$
For the nonzero vacuum $w=-2M/g$ in the shifted coordinate, $M_v=M+gw=-M$. Thus it is also $|-M\widetilde\varphi+g\widetilde\varphi^2/2|^2$. The cubic interaction changes sign compared with expansion at the other root, whereas the quadratic mass term is the same.

= f
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=f}

The canonical complex-scalar <kinetic term> is $\partial_\mu\widetilde\varphi^*\partial^\mu\widetilde\varphi$. Write $\widetilde\varphi=(a+ib)/\sqrt2$ so that $a,b$ each have real canonical normalization. The quadratic potential is $|M_v|^2(a^2+b^2)/2$, giving equal squared masses for both real modes:
$$
M_v^2=(m+gv_\pm)^2=m^2-2g\kappa=\Delta,
\qquad
\boxed{m_{\rm scalar}^2=|\Delta|,\qquad
m_{\rm scalar}=\sqrt{|m^2-2g\kappa|}.}
$$
The first equality is an algebraic square of the generally complex mass parameter; the physical squared mass is its modulus squared. In particular it is not a negative squared mass when real parameters have $\Delta<0$: the vacuum then lies off the original real slice. The constant $\alpha$ does not enter. For $g=0,m\ne0$ this reduces to $|m|$; at a double critical point it is zero.

= g
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=g}

The remaining non-derivative component term in the Lagrangian is the <Yukawa interaction>
$$
\mathcal L_{\rm fermion,pot}
=-\frac12W''(v+\widetilde\varphi)\psi\psi+\mathrm{h.c.}
=-\frac12(M_v+g\widetilde\varphi)\psi\psi+\mathrm{h.c.}
$$
Equivalently the interaction Hamiltonian has the opposite displayed sign. The first term is the two-component <fermion> mass, and the second its scalar-fermion interaction. A phase redefinition of $\psi$ makes the mass coefficient nonnegative, so the physical <Weyl spinor> mass is
$$
\boxed{m_{\rm fermion}=|M_v|=\sqrt{|m^2-2g\kappa|}.}
$$
It is again independent of $\alpha$, the choice of root and any sign of a real $M_v$. This distinguishes the complex bilinear coefficient from the nonnegative physical mass. In the pure-linear supersymmetry-breaking branch $W''=0$, the <fermion> is the massless <Goldstino>.

= h
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=h}

For a nonzero common mass,
$$
\boxed{\frac{m_{\rm scalar}}{m_{\rm fermion}}=1.}
$$
This agrees with <supersymmetric mass degeneracy> in an unbroken <chiral superfield> multiplet. The two real scalar modes have the same mass as the two physical polarizations of the massive <fermion>, as the rest-frame multiplet construction predicts.

At $\Delta=0$ both masses vanish; the numerical ratio is $0/0$ and is undefined, but the equality of masses still expresses the unbroken <supersymmetry>. Equal zero masses in the exceptional flat, pure-linear breaking model do not prove unbroken <supersymmetry>: there the nonzero auxiliary expectation and positive energy in part (c) are the decisive tests.

= 3
{parent=Paper 45}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=3}

First use the actual PDF boost bracket $[K_i,K_j]=-i\epsilon_{ijk}J_k$; replacing the final $J_k$ by $K_k$, as the TeX transcription does, would be a different and incorrect algebra. Expanding the three brackets yields
$$
\begin{aligned}
[A_i,A_j]&=\tfrac14([J_i,J_j]+i[J_i,K_j]+i[K_i,J_j]-[K_i,K_j])
=i\epsilon_{ijk}A_k,\\
[B_i,B_j]&=i\epsilon_{ijk}B_k,\\
[A_i,B_j]&=\tfrac14([J_i,J_j]-i[J_i,K_j]+i[K_i,J_j]+[K_i,K_j])=0.
\end{aligned}
$$
Thus
$$
\boxed{\mathfrak{so}(1,3)_{\mathbb C}
\cong\mathfrak{sl}_2(\mathbb C)\oplus\mathfrak{sl}_2(\mathbb C),
\qquad\mathbf J=\mathbf A+\mathbf B.}
$$
The two sets have SU(2)-type brackets. Their compact real form exponentiates to $SU(2)\times SU(2)$, whereas their full complexification exponentiates to $SL(2,\mathbb C)\times SL(2,\mathbb C)$. This does not turn the physical real <Lorentz group> into a compact product. With Hermitian $J,K$, $A^\dagger=B$, and the two factors are tied by the Lorentz reality condition; the connected physical <spin> cover is $SL(2,\mathbb C)$.

For the right-handed spinor representation, the supplied sigma formula gives $\bar\sigma^{ij}=\epsilon_{ijk}\sigma_k/2$ and $\bar\sigma^{0i}=i\sigma_i/2$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{J_i=\frac{\sigma_i}{2},\qquad K_i=\frac{i\sigma_i}{2},
\qquad A_i=0,\quad B_i=\frac{\sigma_i}{2}.}
$$
It is the $(0,1/2)$ representation in this definition of $A,B$. These are finite-dimensional representation matrices; the boosts are not Hermitian for a positive-definite spinor inner product, as expected for a noncompact group. They are distinct from the operator-component <commutator> coefficients discussed in Question 1.

For the group map, encode a real Minkowski vector by the Hermitian matrix
$$
X=x_\mu\sigma^\mu=x^\nu\sigma_\nu,
\qquad\det X=(x^0)^2-|\mathbf x|^2.
$$
The trace identity $\tfrac12\operatorname{Tr}(\bar\sigma^\mu\sigma_\nu)=\delta^\mu{}_{\nu}$ recovers its contravariant components. For $N\in SL(2,\mathbb C)$, $X'=NXN^\dagger$ is Hermitian and has the same determinant. Expanding $X'$ in that basis gives
$$
\boxed{x'^\mu=\Lambda^\mu{}_{\nu}(N)x^\nu,
\qquad\Lambda^\mu{}_{\nu}(N)
=\frac12\operatorname{Tr}(\bar\sigma^\mu N\sigma_\nu N^\dagger).}
$$
The coefficients are real because the trace of a product of Hermitian matrices is real. Equality of determinants for all $x$ proves $\Lambda^T\eta\Lambda=\eta$. The action for $N_1N_2$ is the composition of the two actions, proving the homomorphism law.

Since $SL(2,\mathbb C)$ is connected and the identity maps to the identity, its image has determinant $+1$ and preserves time orientation. More directly, positive-definite matrices representing future timelike vectors remain positive definite under $X\mapsto NXN^\dagger$. The kernel is $\{I,-I\}$: if every Hermitian $X$ is fixed, $X=I$ first makes $N$ unitary, and then commutation with all Hermitian matrices makes it a scalar with determinant one.

Conversely, $SU(2)$ gives every spatial rotation, and positive Hermitian determinant-one matrices $\exp(\mathbf b\cdot\boldsymbol\sigma/2)$ give every pure boost. Rotation-boost decomposition yields every proper orthochronous <Lorentz transformation>. Thus the formula gives the <Lorentz spinor double cover>
$$
\boxed{SL(2,\mathbb C)/\{\pm I\}\cong SO^+(1,3).}
$$
It is a map into $SO(1,3)$ as asked, with precisely its identity component as image; it does not produce disconnected time-reversing transformations.

= a
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The Weyl fields have odd <Grassmann parity>. With the given epsilon convention, write $\chi_\alpha=(a,b)$ and $\psi_\alpha=(c,d)$, where all four components anticommute. Raising an index gives $\chi^\alpha=(b,-a)$ and $\psi^\alpha=(d,-c)$. Hence
$$
(\chi\psi)=bc-ad,
\qquad(\psi\chi)=da-cb=-ad+bc.
$$
The minus sign from the antisymmetric epsilon tensor is canceled by exchanging the two Grassmann-odd components. Therefore the <Weyl spinor bilinear exchange identity> is
$$
\boxed{(\chi\psi)=(\psi\chi),\qquad C=1.}
$$
For commuting numerical spinors the exchange sign would instead be negative. The answer here uses fermionic Weyl fields, as appropriate to the supersymmetric model.

= b
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Use the same four Grassmann generators and a fixed order $a,b,c,d$. Their squares vanish, so
$$
(bc-ad)^2=-bcad-adbc=-2abcd.
$$
Meanwhile the two self-contractions are
$$
(\psi\psi)=dc-cd=-2cd,\qquad
(\chi\chi)=ba-ab=-2ab,
$$
and moving the pair $cd$ past $ab$ gives no sign, yielding $(\psi\psi)(\chi\chi)=4abcd$. Thus the <square of a Weyl spinor bilinear> is
$$
\boxed{(\chi\psi)^2=-\frac12(\psi\psi)(\chi\chi),\qquad D=-\frac12.}
$$
The order of individual odd components, rather than an informal commuting-spinor substitution, fixes this sign. For commuting spinors both self-contractions vanish, so no corresponding universal factorization of the generally nonzero mixed square is possible.
